WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:01.310
Remember Pebble?

00:00:01.310 --> 00:00:05.299
The smartwatch pioneer that had beat all the
tech giants to the punch?

00:00:05.299 --> 00:00:10.280
Between 2012 and 2015, Pebble was one of the
most popular smart watch brands out there.

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:15.270
And they had a massive fanbase that supported
them against challengers like Apple and Samsung.

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:19.950
Yet within just 15 months, they went from
being the dominating player to being a large

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:22.600
player to being a laggard to shutting down.

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:26.540
Their entire fall from glory didn’t take
much longer than just a year.

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:30.800
So, what happened to Pebble?

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.480
Taking a look back, Pebble wasn’t some sort
of big tech company or some billionaire's

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:36.120
passion project.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:40.670
Pebble was actually a startup founded by Eric
Migicovsky through Y combinator and Kickstarter.

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:44.739
At Y Combinator, Eric’s smartwatch idea
did catch the attention of a couple of venture

00:00:44.739 --> 00:00:47.260
capitalists who invested a total of $375,000.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:52.970
But though that was a solid amount, it was
nowhere near enough to hire hardware designers,

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:57.260
software engineers, app developers, and everyone
else involved in creating a smartwatch from

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:58.260
scratch.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:02.230
The problem was that while most investors
were intrigued by the idea, they weren’t

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:06.289
willing to invest into the project because
Pebble was largely a hardware business.

00:01:06.289 --> 00:01:09.780
And hardware businesses are notorious for
having razor thin profit margins and large

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:11.310
capital requirements.

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:15.759
Unable to convince more VCs, Eric decided
to take his idea to Kickstarter and this is

00:01:15.759 --> 00:01:17.930
where the brand would really take shape.

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:21.399
Tech enthusiasts on Kickstarter had no care
for the business side of Pebble.

00:01:21.399 --> 00:01:25.659
They had fantasized about smartwatches for
years, and someone was finally offering to

00:01:25.659 --> 00:01:26.729
make it a reality.

00:01:26.729 --> 00:01:31.060
Not to mention, Pebble’s offerings were
super cheap especially compared to today’s

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:32.060
standards.

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:37.189
Their retail price was projected to be $150,
but they were offering it for $115 to Kickstarter

00:01:37.189 --> 00:01:40.329
backers and $100 for the first 200 backers.

00:01:40.329 --> 00:01:45.179
In April of 2012, Eric launched the fundraiser
with a target of $100,000.

00:01:45.179 --> 00:01:48.280
He would end up raising the entire amount
within just 2 hours.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:52.469
And over the next 6 days, Pebble became the
most funded project in Kickstarter’s history

00:01:52.469 --> 00:01:55.130
raising over $4.7 million.

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:59.759
By the end of the campaign, Pebble was able
to raise $10.2 million, and with that they

00:01:59.759 --> 00:02:01.509
were off to the races.

00:02:01.509 --> 00:02:05.499
Pebble began working with a consulting firm
called Dragon Innovation who helped them identify

00:02:05.499 --> 00:02:08.179
suitable suppliers and manufacturers.

00:02:08.179 --> 00:02:13.080
The next several months were not easy by any
means as Pebble ran into a plethora of manufacturing

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:14.980
difficulties and design limitations.

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:18.870
But, Eric slowly addressed one issue after
another and he was eventually able to launch

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:21.210
the product in early 2013.

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:25.540
The watch was quite simple and nothing fancy,
but what made the pebble watch stick out was

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:27.310
its insane utility.

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:32.260
Pebble supported email, call, and text message
notifications, and you could check stock prices,

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:34.110
track workouts, and monitor your sleep.

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:39.560
It was basically just a standard smart watch,
but in 2013, this was phenomenal.

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:43.130
And the best part was that since Pebble opted
for a minimal energy black and white display,

00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:45.760
they were able to offer 7 day battery life.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:51.150
For $100 to $150, backers were extremely impressed,
and the pebble watch launched to an uproar

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:53.350
of positive reviews and feedback.

00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:56.320
Eric didn’t let any of this get to his head
though, he was laser focused on pushing out

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:59.180
more applications and improving the customer
experience.

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:04.690
By February of 2014, the pebble app store
boasted a total of 1000 applications, but

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:06.790
it wasn’t all smooth sailing.

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:10.770
Notable players like Samsung and Motorola
were starting to roll out their own smart

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:14.990
watches, so Eric decided to fire back with
a 2nd generation called the Pebble Time.

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:18.430
Eric had been closely monitoring all of the
constructive feedback he received on the original

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:22.630
Pebble watch, and he addressed basically every
concern with the Pebble Time.

00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:27.319
In terms of hardware, the time offered a 64
color display and it sported gorilla glass.

00:03:27.319 --> 00:03:31.110
The time also allowed users to swap the stock
straps with third party straps.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:34.630
Eric also introduced premium versions of the
time called the pebble time steel and the

00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:36.160
pebble time round.

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:39.960
The pebble time steel offered higher quality
finishes including steel and leather, and

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:41.850
it offered a 10 day battery life.

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:45.860
Meanwhile, the pebble time round offered a
more traditional looking watch.

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:50.710
In February of 2015, Eric once again went
to Kickstarter to fund this launch, and Pebble

00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:52.300
would obliterate every record.

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:54.510
Their goal was to raise $500,000.

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:59.959
They would reach this goal in just 17 minutes,
and they would reach a million dollars within

00:03:59.959 --> 00:04:01.370
49 minutes.

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:06.390
By the end of the fundraiser, they raised
a total of $20 million.

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:10.080
Apple would reveal the Apple watch a few months
later, and Pebble fans laughed at it.

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:15.750
Apple expected people to pay $350 up to $17,000
for a product that was comparable to Pebble’s

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:16.750
offerings.

00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:20.430
Not to mention, the Apple watch only had a
battery life of 18 hours compared to Pebble’s

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:22.509
7 to 10 day long batteries.

00:04:22.509 --> 00:04:25.580
Eric was actually excited for the launch of
the Apple watch.

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:28.749
He anticipated that the Apple watch would
simply increase the interest in smartwatches

00:04:28.749 --> 00:04:30.610
and drive up Pebble sales.

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:32.370
And he was absolutely right.

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:35.680
In the 6 months following the release of the
Apple watch, Pebble’s sales doubled.

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:41.050
They even turned down a $740 million acquisition
offer from watchmaker Citizen.

00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:44.970
But just 15 months later, the company would
shut down.

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:46.849
So what in the world happened?

00:04:46.849 --> 00:04:51.289
Well, I think the first and biggest issue
bringing down pebble was their laser focus

00:04:51.289 --> 00:04:52.669
on utility.

00:04:52.669 --> 00:04:57.030
From day one, Pebble was never trying to produce
a trendy or lifestyle product.

00:04:57.030 --> 00:05:00.240
They were trying to create the most value
packed hardware they could.

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:04.300
They could’ve easily thrown on an LED screen
and cool animations, but they prioritized

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:06.770
battery life over flashy looks.

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:10.650
At first, this was the perfect approach because
the entire smart watch community was filled

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:14.580
with a bunch of tech enthusiasts whose primary
focus was utility.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:18.379
All they wanted was a long lasting smartwatch
that could display notifications and useful

00:05:18.379 --> 00:05:19.379
information.

00:05:19.379 --> 00:05:20.379
That’s it.

00:05:20.379 --> 00:05:22.879
They weren’t looking to flex their watch
and they couldn’t care less about what others

00:05:22.879 --> 00:05:24.310
thought about it.

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:28.479
But as Apple introduced the Apple watch, the
general public got involved, and their expectations

00:05:28.479 --> 00:05:30.229
were radically different.

00:05:30.229 --> 00:05:34.719
The average person buying an Apple watch doesn’t
actually use it for work related purposes.

00:05:34.719 --> 00:05:37.819
They're not tracking stocks throughout the
day and it’s probably been months since

00:05:37.819 --> 00:05:39.650
they sent their last email.

00:05:39.650 --> 00:05:44.409
The top two reasons that people buy an Apple
watch is for raw convenience and looks.

00:05:44.409 --> 00:05:47.371
They don’t want to pull out their phone
to view text messages and they don’t want

00:05:47.371 --> 00:05:50.180
their phone shaking around in their pocket
while they’re working out.

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:54.240
Oh yeah, and they like to see the little activity
circles fill up throughout the day.

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:58.860
They don’t care about a 7 day battery life
and they don’t want to look at a bland display.

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:01.499
They want rich colors and an intriguing UI.

00:06:01.499 --> 00:06:05.479
And above all, whether they want to admit
it or not, they want a flex.

00:06:05.479 --> 00:06:10.270
Apple likes to brand themselves as a lifestyle
brand, but we all know that this just means

00:06:10.270 --> 00:06:11.699
that they’re a flex brand.

00:06:11.699 --> 00:06:16.300
Now, I can’t really blame these guys cause
I was basically describing myself right there.

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:18.990
But this was really bad news for Pebble.

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:22.939
The smartwatch space was rapidly growing,
but all of the consumers entering the space

00:06:22.939 --> 00:06:24.559
weren’t tech enthusiasts.

00:06:24.559 --> 00:06:28.530
They were general consumers who didn’t really
care all that much about utility.

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:31.949
But then, why was Pebble’s sales skyrocketing
after the Apple Watch announcement?

00:06:31.949 --> 00:06:36.729
Well, I think this was just an impulse reaction
to Apple’s crazy pricing.

00:06:36.729 --> 00:06:41.439
Spending $350 to $400 on an Apple watch seemed
ludicrous when there were similar offerings

00:06:41.439 --> 00:06:43.419
for less than half the price.

00:06:43.419 --> 00:06:46.740
And that brings me into the second reason
behind Pebble’s downfall which is a race

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:48.219
to the bottom.

00:06:48.219 --> 00:06:51.150
After the launch of the Apple watch, the number
one thing that still attracted the general

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:54.039
public towards pebble watches was price.

00:06:54.039 --> 00:06:57.430
Other than battery life, the Apple watch was
slightly better than pebble’s offerings

00:06:57.430 --> 00:06:58.580
in every way.

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:00.559
Now, was it twice as good?

00:07:00.559 --> 00:07:01.559
Not even close.

00:07:01.559 --> 00:07:05.719
But, it was slightly better and it was tied
into the Apple ecosystem.

00:07:05.719 --> 00:07:09.169
This resulted in people buying pebble watches
as a cheaper alternative that offered more

00:07:09.169 --> 00:07:10.789
value per dollar.

00:07:10.789 --> 00:07:14.500
In other words, the general public wasn’t
buying pebble watches because they were enthusiastic

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:16.590
about the brand or products.

00:07:16.590 --> 00:07:19.120
They were simply settling with a pebble watch.

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:22.660
And the worst part was that Pebble was simply
encouraging people to upgrade to an Apple

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:23.660
watch.

00:07:23.660 --> 00:07:27.610
As people wore pebble watches, they realized
how convenient a smart watch was, and the

00:07:27.610 --> 00:07:30.599
next time they bought a smart watch, many
of them decided to splurge and spring for

00:07:30.599 --> 00:07:31.780
an Apple watch.

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:35.719
So, Pebble was almost serving as an entry
level product that got people hooked.

00:07:35.719 --> 00:07:39.069
To make things even worse, Apple started to
be more competitive.

00:07:39.069 --> 00:07:43.219
IPhone sales had hit a peak in the mid 2010s,
and Apple started taking diversification much

00:07:43.219 --> 00:07:44.569
more seriously.

00:07:44.569 --> 00:07:49.530
They offered much lower entry prices and they
kept older generations around for much longer.

00:07:49.530 --> 00:07:54.249
You can literally get a brand new Apple Watch
series 3 from their official website for $200.

00:07:54.249 --> 00:07:57.569
And if you wait for black Friday, you can
easily pick up an Apple Watch for less than

00:07:57.569 --> 00:07:58.999
$150.

00:07:58.999 --> 00:08:03.539
Now of course, this isn’t the flagship version,
but I’d be willing to bet that most people

00:08:03.539 --> 00:08:07.110
would take an older generation Apple Watch
over a newer generation Pebble watch if they

00:08:07.110 --> 00:08:08.599
were roughly the same price.

00:08:08.599 --> 00:08:12.680
So, as Apple brought out newer generations,
it just became cheaper and cheaper to get

00:08:12.680 --> 00:08:14.159
an Apple watch.

00:08:14.159 --> 00:08:17.879
And given that price was the only thing that
attracted the general public to Pebble watches,

00:08:17.879 --> 00:08:20.180
they left as soon as Apple met that need.

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:23.990
And this was made extremely clear in the holiday
season of 2015.

00:08:23.990 --> 00:08:27.800
Up until then, the release of the Apple watch
had been boosting Pebble sales.

00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:31.240
But as soon as Apple watches were being offered
for substantial discounts throughout the holiday

00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:33.310
season, Pebble sales plummeted.

00:08:33.310 --> 00:08:38.180
In the last quarter of 2015, Pebble went from
doubling their sales in the last six months

00:08:38.180 --> 00:08:40.390
to negative year over year growth.

00:08:40.390 --> 00:08:43.700
And let’s keep in mind, Pebble wasn’t
a profitable business.

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:46.830
They were still in the growth phase and they
needed to achieve economies of scale to produce

00:08:46.830 --> 00:08:48.690
sustainable profitability.

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:51.260
If growth was slowing down, they were screwed.

00:08:51.260 --> 00:08:55.040
Eric tried to address this decline in demand
with the Pebble 2 which debuted on Kickstarter

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:56.930
in May of 2016.

00:08:56.930 --> 00:09:00.990
They still performed extremely well on Kickstarter,
but the excitement was substantially lower

00:09:00.990 --> 00:09:02.590
than last time.

00:09:02.590 --> 00:09:07.220
Pebble was able to raise $12.7 million and
while that’s an insane amount, that’s

00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:09.140
40% less than last time.

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:13.270
And for a startup that needed consistently
increasing demand, this was the end.

00:09:13.270 --> 00:09:17.540
Eric scrambled to find a company that could
acquire Pebble, but by this point, Pebble’s

00:09:17.540 --> 00:09:19.910
disappointing outlook was pretty clear.

00:09:19.910 --> 00:09:24.800
The only company willing to buy Pebble was
Fitbit, and their offer came at just $40 million,

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:28.420
$700 million less than just a year ago.

00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:31.530
Fitbit wasn’t looking to save Pebble or
keep the brand alive.

00:09:31.530 --> 00:09:34.170
They were simply trying to leverage Pebble’s
software and their team.

00:09:34.170 --> 00:09:38.600
So, as soon as Fitbit acquired Pebble, they
fired everyone except for the software team

00:09:38.600 --> 00:09:41.430
and shut down the company at the end of 2016.

00:09:41.430 --> 00:09:43.030
And that was the end of Pebble.

00:09:43.030 --> 00:09:47.480
Honestly, there wasn’t really anything that
Pebble could’ve done better.

00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:52.190
Sure, they could’ve tended to the desire
of the general public, but I don’t think

00:09:52.190 --> 00:09:53.790
that would’ve played out much better either.

00:09:53.790 --> 00:09:57.880
Pebble’s monumental growth was thanks to
a lack of options along with great execution

00:09:57.880 --> 00:09:59.200
by Eric.

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:04.130
But the truth is, no level of execution could’ve
overpowered Apple in the mid 2010s.

00:10:04.130 --> 00:10:06.990
Truly a sad ending to a spectacular start.

